# Cisco 2921 — SSH Remote Access Configuration

## Project Overview

I configured SSH on my Cisco 2921 router so I could access and manage it remotely from my computer using PuTTY.

Before setting up SSH, I was using a console cable to access the router. I wanted to be able to log in through the network without having to connect the console cable every time.

## Equipment Used

- Cisco 2921 router
- Cisco switch
- Windows computer
- PuTTY
- Console cable

## Network Information

Router IP address: 192.168.1.1

SSH username: CCNA

SSH port: 22

SSH version: 2

## SSH Configuration

I used the router's local user account for SSH authentication and configured the VTY lines to allow remote access.

The main commands used for the SSH setup are shown below.

```cisco
configure terminal

hostname CCNA

ip domain-name homelab.local

username CCNA privilege 15 secret <password>

crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048

ip ssh version 2

line vty 0 15
 login local
 transport input ssh
exit
```

The RSA keys are used by the router during SSH authentication and connection setup. I configured SSH version 2 instead of allowing the older SSH version 1.

I also configured the VTY lines to use the local username and password and accept SSH connections.

## Testing the SSH Connection

After configuring SSH, I opened PuTTY on my Windows computer.

I entered the router's IP address, `192.168.1.1`, selected SSH, and connected using port 22.

PuTTY opened a terminal session where I could enter my username and password.

I was able to log in and access the Cisco router remotely.

## Verifying SSH

I checked the SSH configuration using:

```cisco
show ip ssh
```

The router displayed:

```text
SSH Enabled - version 2.0
Authentication timeout: 120 secs; Authentication retries: 3
Minimum expected Diffie Hellman key size : 1024 bits
```

This confirmed that SSH version 2 was enabled.

I also used the following command to check the VTY configuration:

```cisco
show running-config | section line vty
```

## Final Result

I was able to connect to my Cisco 2921 router through PuTTY using SSH.

The router could now be managed remotely over the network without needing a console connection for normal configuration tasks.

## What I Learned

This project helped me understand how SSH is used for remote management of Cisco devices.

I learned the purpose of RSA keys, local user authentication, SSH version 2, and VTY lines.

I also learned the difference between accessing a router through a console cable and accessing it remotely through an IP network.

Being able to manage the router through SSH made working on my physical home lab more convenient.
